Here's a Java implementation of the binary search algorithm:

```java
public class BinarySearch {

    /**
     * Binary search on a sorted array. Returns index or -1 if not found.
     * Time complexity: O(log n), Space complexity: O(1)
     */
    public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;  // Prevents overflow

            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                return mid;  // Found target at index mid
            } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
                left = mid + 1;  // Search right half
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;  // Search left half
            }
        }

        return -1;  // Target not found
    }

    // Generic version for any Comparable type:
    public static <T extends Comparable<T>> int binarySearch(T[] arr, T target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            int cmp = arr[mid].compareTo(target);

            if (cmp == 0) return mid;
            else if (cmp < 0) left = mid + 1;
            else right = mid - 1;
        }

        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] sortedArray = {2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 45, 56, 72};
        int target = 23;
        int result = binarySearch(sortedArray, target);
        System.out.println("Found " + target + " at index: " + result);  // Output: 5

        target = 100;
        result = binarySearch(sortedArray, target);
        System.out.println("Found " + target + " at index: " + result);  // Output: -1
    }
}
```

Binary search requires a sorted array and works by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half. The mid calculation uses `left + (right - left) / 2` instead of `(left + right) / 2` to prevent integer overflow for large arrays.
